@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+# Prouhet--Tarry--Escott witness generator (arXiv:2506.11429)
+
+| profile | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — no-tool compression |
+| Native domain | number theory |
+| Object regime | integer lattice |
+| Computational core | subset sum |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (one actual PTE array) |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: recognize an affine Boolean subset-sum cube and its parity classes |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## Problem and trust model
+
+This module instantiates Section 1.1 of Chen Shuwen, [*A survey of The Prouhet-Tarry-Escott Problem and its Generalizations*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.11429). The solver receives 32 distinct non-negative integers and must choose 16 whose sums of powers 1, 2, 3, and 4 equal those of the complementary 16. The submitted witness is the integer array itself. `verify` checks membership, cardinality, distinctness, and all four power-sum identities with exact Python integers; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+Generation is by composition of identities, not solution search. For five sampled positive weights, all 32 subset sums form a Boolean cube. Splitting those sums by subset-size parity makes the signed exponential generating function `product(1-exp(w_i*t))`, whose coefficients through degree four vanish. A common integer dilation and translation preserve PTE identities exactly, as stated immediately after equation (1.2) in Section 1.1; a final permutation only changes presentation. The two planted sides therefore come from the same distribution, with no decoys or distinguished planted elements.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+This is not a Track A claim. Section 1.1 already credits Prouhet with existence in every degree, gives the affine construction that makes transformed instances easy once recognized, and identifies `m <= degree` as the no-nontrivial-solution boundary. Chapter 6 explicitly develops computer search for GPTE/PTE instances. For this generated distribution, the included generic balanced meet-in-the-middle solver tracks cardinality and four moment sums in `O(4*2^(N/2))` exact time and `O(2^(N/2))` memory. Across eight shipping-preset seeds it solved 8/8, using at most 645,448 counted operations and 0.224330 seconds. That is the expected reference success.
+
+The compact route is different: subtract the minimum, obtain the common affine scale as a gcd, reconstruct the five positive subset generators from the complete subset-sum multiset, and use parity. The measured maximum is 188 exact arithmetic operations. That fits the no-tool cap but depends on recognizing the invariant; blind balanced-subset search does not.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+At `demo`, seed 7, the complete rendered data are:
+
+```text
+Choose 4 of these 8 values so that the chosen half and its complement have equal
+sums and equal sums of squares:
+1: 506   2: 519   3: 753   4: 532
+5: 987   6: 766   7: 285   8: 740
+```
+
+The answer is `[285, 740, 753, 766]`. `verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`; dropping the last value returns `(False, "answer has 3 entries; expected exactly 4")`. A person can solve this setting on paper: normalization gives the subset sums of 17, 18, and 19, and the even-parity sums are 0, 35, 36, and 37.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | weight bits `n` | cube dimension | affine-scale bits | integers | answer atoms | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 5 | 3 | 4 | 8 | 4 | hand example |
+| easy | 48 | 5 | 48 | 32 | 16 | configured shipping preset; oracle evidence pending |
+| medium | 72 | 5 | 80 | 32 | 16 | available escalation |
+| hard | 96 | 5 | 112 | 32 | 16 | available escalation |
+
+Both numeric bit-length dials grow while the 16-element answer length stays fixed. `escalate` continues those two axes and reports `cap_bound` before serialized integer length would cross the output cap.
+
+## Local gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses, every preset and three seeds |
+| G2 | pass | drop, replacement, duplicate, empty, and nonmember all rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged prose plus fenced JSON round-trips exactly |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 legal balanced random halves; space = 601,080,390 |
+| G5 | pass | shipping sampled density 0/200,000; demo exact valid count = 2; reference maximum 645,448 operations |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks at 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8; compact route 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=96` builds, verifies, and keeps 16 answer atoms |
+| G8 | pass | 100/100 invariance and carried-certificate checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 511 characters, about 128 tokens, 16 atoms, 188 intended-route operations |
+| G9(b) | **pending** | OpenRouter rejected every harness attempt with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` |
+
+## Adversaries and oracle loop
+
+| attack | successes/attempts |
+|---|---:|
+| choose both magnitude extremes | 0/8 |
+| largest-first first-moment balancing | 0/8 |
+| 256 uniform balanced restarts | 0/8 |
+| alternating values after sorting | 0/8 |
+| meet-in-the-middle reference (expected success) | 8/8 |
+
+No bare oracle attempt has been scored. The harness made four redraws at `easy` (seeds 1580359355, 434248375, 599540796, and 1234272118), and every call was an HTTP 403 account-limit error. Per `harden.py`, errors are not model failures and cannot support a hardness claim. Consequently the bare/hinted/placebo G9 table is not yet available, `G9_ORACLE_RESULTS["hinted_verdict"]` remains `"pending"`, and `selftest_report.json` honestly has `all_passed: false` until a funded OpenRouter key is supplied.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2506_11429 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+print(render(inst))
+ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+```
+
+After the oracle blocker is cleared, emit shipping records from the repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2506.11429 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The observed 0/200,000 guessing rate is only an upper-resolution sampled density under the declared prior: uniform choice among all 16-of-32 halves. It is not evidence against construction-aware algorithms. In fact, affine-cube reconstruction solves every generated instance quickly on a machine, which is why the family is explicitly Track B. Large integer coefficients—not asymptotic NP-hardness—create the gap between mechanical execution and a no-tool response. The panel did not run an ILP/SMT package or lattice reduction; meet-in-the-middle is the strongest implemented generic exact search, and the construction-specific recovery routine is also measured. Finally, external oracle and G9(b) evidence is absent because of the OpenRouter account limit, so this directory is not submission-ready despite all local gates passing.
