87:\subsection{The Prouhet-Tarry-Escott Problem (PTE)}
88:The study of the Prouhet-Tarry-Escott problem can be traced back to the period of 1750-1751, when Euler and Goldbach observed the case for the second power \cite{Dickson52}. In 1851, Prouhet demonstrated that integer solutions exist for any power, provided that the number of variables in the arrays is sufficiently large \cite{Wright1959}. In the 1910s, E. B. Escott and G. Tarry found partial solutions for powers up to the seventh \cite{Gloden44}. Consequently, the problem is named after these three individuals \cite{Borwein1994}. For further references on the Prouhet-Tarry-Escott problem, see \cite{Guy04, Hardy38, Hua, Raghavendran2019}, and other relevant works.
